# Report Generation Agent network built with the neuro-san framework.
#
# Implements the following architecture:
#           |
#   JSON schema input  + Query             (aliases, real names, explanations)
#           |
#   Alias context builder           (schema_alias_tool: strips real names,
#           |                        hands the LLM aliases + explanations only)
#   SQL generator agent  <--------.  (drafts SQL using ALIASES only)
#           |                     |
#   SQL validator agent  --------'  (cross-checked by LLM B; loops back to
#           |                        generator on failure; max 3 retries)
#   Security and compliance agent   (checks aliased query for risk)
#           |
#   Alias mapping (code, not LLM)   (sqlite_query_tool: deterministic sqlglot
#           |                        rewrite of aliases back to real names)
#   MCP server [SIMULATED]          (sqlite_query_tool acts as a CodedTool
#           |                        stand-in; in production this step would
#           |                        call a real MCP server exposing SQLite)
#   SQLite database                 (executes query, returns rows)
#           |
#   Xlsx writer step (XlsxWriterTool)  (formats rows into workbook)
#           |
#   Report Generated   #                                       count to the user via a plain-language
#                                       message; no live ITIL API integration —
#                                       ITIL ticket closure is manual/external)
#
# Alias mapping:
# The real database/table/column names are never shown to the LLM. Instead,
# BH_report_agent/data/schema_aliases.json defines an alias + human-readable
# explanation for each module, table, and column. schema_alias_tool
# hands that alias view to sql_generator_agent, which composes SQL using
# ALIASES only. sqlite_query_tool then performs a DETERMINISTIC, code-only
# (not LLM) rewrite of the alias-based SQL back to the real names using a
# sqlglot AST pass (via coded_tools/schema_alias_map.py) before executing
# it against the actual SQLite database.
#
# MCP server note:
# sqlite_query_tool.py is a CodedTool that stands in for an MCP server. It
# handles alias translation, SELECT-only validation, and direct SQLite
# execution locally. To connect a real MCP server in future, replace this
# CodedTool with an MCP client call while keeping the alias-translation step.
#
# ITIL integration note:
# The report_orchestrator notifies the user with the report file path and row
# count so the ITIL ticket can be closed, but does NOT call any ITIL/ITSM API.
# Actual ticket closure requires a separate integration (e.g. a ServiceNow
# CodedTool) to be added to the pipeline.
#
# Schema reference:
# https://github.com/cognizant-ai-lab/neuro-san/blob/main/docs/agent_hocon_reference.md

{
    "llm_config": {
        "model_name": "us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0",
        "class": "anthropic-bedrock",
        "region_name": "us-west-2",
        "aws_access_key_id": "YOUR_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID",
        "aws_secret_access_key": "YOUR_AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY",
        "aws_session_token": "YOUR_AWS_SESSION_TOKEN_IF_USING_STS"
    },

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Report generation agent network for ITIL service requests: drafts and validates SQL, enforces security/compliance, queries a SQLite database via an MCP-style tool, and formats the results into an Xlsx report.",
        "tags": ["report-generation", "sql", "itil", "bh_report_agent"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Generate a report of all open incidents assigned to the network team in the last 30 days.",
            "Pull a list of employees in the Finance department along with their email addresses."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        # Front-man agent: receives the ITIL adhoc service request (as a JSON
        # schema / natural-language description of the desired report) and
        # orchestrates the rest of the pipeline.
        {
            "name": "report_orchestrator",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I generate data reports in response to ITIL adhoc service requests.
Describe the report you need (e.g. what data, filters, and date ranges),
and optionally provide a JSON schema describing the expected input fields.
I will draft and validate the SQL, check security/compliance, run the query,
and deliver an Xlsx report.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "request": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Natural-language or JSON-schema description of the ITIL adhoc service request / report needed."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["request"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
You are the front-man agent for a report-generation pipeline that responds to
ITIL adhoc service requests.

Given the user's request (which may include a JSON schema describing the
expected input fields):

1. Call schema_alias_tool (the "alias context builder") to load the
   available module/table/column ALIASES and their metadata. This strips all
   real names so the LLM only ever sees aliases. Always pass the user's
   request text as the "user_query" argument so the tool returns only the
   tables relevant to that request (it falls back to the full schema when
   no tables match). Do this once at the start (and again if
   sql_generator_agent indicates it needs the schema re-sent).
2. Call sql_generator_agent with the request and the alias schema view from
   step 1, asking it to draft a read-only SQL SELECT statement expressed
   ENTIRELY in terms of the provided aliases (never real table/column names,
   which you don't have access to anyway).
3. Take the SQL produced and call sql_validator_agent, passing along both the
   SQL and the alias schema view, to have it cross-checked.
   If sql_validator_agent reports the SQL is invalid or unsafe, send its
   feedback back to sql_generator_agent to refine the SQL, and repeat this
   validation loop (steps 2-3) until the SQL is approved. Do not loop more
   than 3 times; if still not approved, explain the problem to the user
   instead of proceeding.
4. Once validated, call security_and_compliance_agent with the approved
   alias-based SQL to check access rights and injection risk. If it flags a
   problem, stop and report the issue to the user rather than executing the
   query.
5. Once cleared, call sqlite_query_tool with the approved alias-based SQL
   (unchanged - do NOT translate the aliases yourself). The tool performs a
   deterministic, code-only (not LLM) sqlglot rewrite of the aliases back to
   the real column/table names and executes it against the database. If
   the tool returns an "error" key, explain the problem clearly to the user
   and stop.
6. Call xlsx_writer_tool with the returned columns and rows to format them
   into an Xlsx workbook. If it returns an "error" key, explain the problem
   to the user and stop.
7. Report back to the user with the file path of the generated report and a
   short summary (e.g. row count), indicating that the ITIL ticket can be
   closed.

Always be transparent about any step that failed and why, so the user (or the
ITIL ticket) can be updated accordingly.
""",
            "tools": [
                "schema_alias_tool",
                "sql_generator_agent",
                "sql_validator_agent",
                "security_and_compliance_agent",
                "sqlite_query_tool",
                "xlsx_writer_tool"
            ]
        },

        # Down-chain CodedTool: loads the JSON schema-alias definition and
        # returns an alias + explanation view of the database/tables/fields,
        # so downstream agents compose SQL using aliases only.
        {
            "name": "schema_alias_tool",
            "function": {
                "description": "Loads the module/table/column alias definitions and their metadata, for composing SQL using aliases only (never real names). Pass the user's natural-language request as user_query so only relevant tables are returned.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "schema_path": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Optional absolute path to the schema-alias JSON file. Omit this parameter to use the built-in default."
                        },
                        "user_query": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The user's natural-language report request. When provided, only tables relevant to this query are included in the returned schema view (plus FK-referenced tables needed for JOINs). Omit to load the full schema."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": []
                }
            },
            "class": "schema_alias_tool.SchemaAliasTool"
        },

        # SQL generator agent: drafts and refines SQL based on the request
        # and any validator feedback.
        {
            "name": "sql_generator_agent",

            "function": {
                "description": "Drafts or refines a read-only SQL SELECT statement, expressed using schema aliases only, to answer a described report request.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "request": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The report request to translate into SQL."
                        },
                        "schema_view": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "The alias view of the modules/tables/columns returned by schema_alias_tool. SQL must be composed using ONLY these aliases."
                        },
                        "feedback": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Optional feedback from the validator on a previous SQL draft that must be addressed."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["request", "schema_view"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
You are a SQL generation specialist.
You will be given a natural-language report request, an alias schema view
(database/table/field ALIASES with human-readable explanations of what each
one means), and, if provided, feedback from a prior validation attempt.

Produce a single, read-only SQL SELECT statement that answers the request,
using the explanations in the alias schema view to figure out which aliased
tables and fields are relevant.

Rules:
- You do NOT know, and must never guess or invent, the real database, table,
  or column names. Refer to tables and fields using ONLY the "alias" values
  given to you in schema_view (e.g. use the table alias in the FROM clause
  and field aliases in SELECT/WHERE/etc., exactly as given).
- Only ever produce SELECT statements. Never produce INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE,
  DROP, ALTER, CREATE, ATTACH, or PRAGMA statements.
- Never include multiple statements separated by semicolons.
- Prefer explicit field-alias lists over SELECT *.
- Foreign Key JOINs (mandatory): Every column in schema_view that has a
  "join_hint" field is a foreign key. When any such FK column belongs to a
  table you are querying, you MUST follow its join_hint:
    1. Add the indicated LEFT JOIN clause (replacing "<this_col>" with the
       fully qualified alias, e.g. employees.dept_id).
    2. Remove the raw FK ID column from the SELECT list and instead SELECT
       the display fields listed after "fetch:" in the join_hint.
    3. For self-referential FKs (e.g. employees.mgr_id references employees),
       alias the joined table to avoid ambiguity, e.g.:
         LEFT JOIN employees AS mgr ON employees.mgr_id = mgr.emp_id
       then SELECT mgr.fname, mgr.lname.
    4. If the same referenced table must be joined more than once (e.g. both
       dept_id and a manager FK point to employees), use distinct aliases for
       each join.
- If feedback is provided, address every point raised before returning the
  revised SQL.
- Return ONLY the alias-based SQL statement as your final answer, with no
  extra commentary, explanation, or markdown formatting.
"""
        },

        # SQL validator agent: acts as "LLM B", independently cross-checking
        # the SQL for correctness and safety.
        {
            "name": "sql_validator_agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Independently reviews an alias-based SQL SELECT statement for correctness and safety, approving it or returning specific feedback.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "sql": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The alias-based SQL SELECT statement to validate."
                        },
                        "request": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The original report request the SQL is meant to answer."
                        },
                        "schema_view": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "The alias view of the modules/tables/columns returned by schema_alias_tool, used to check the SQL only references known aliases."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["sql", "request", "schema_view"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
You are an independent SQL reviewer (acting as a second, cross-checking model).
Given an alias-based SQL SELECT statement, the original request it is meant
to answer, and the alias schema view it should be composed against, verify
that:
- It is a single, syntactically valid, read-only SELECT statement.
- It does not contain INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, DROP, ALTER, CREATE, ATTACH,
  PRAGMA, or multiple statements.
- Every table/field reference in the SQL is one of the "alias" values present
  in schema_view (i.e. it does NOT reference any real table/column names,
  which would indicate the generator broke the alias contract).
- It plausibly answers the original request (correct aliased tables/fields/
  filters, per their explanations in schema_view).
- For every FK column (any column whose schema_view entry has a "join_hint")
  that belongs to a queried table, a corresponding JOIN is present and the
  display fields from the referenced table (listed after "fetch:" in the
  join_hint) are selected instead of the raw FK ID, unless the request
  explicitly asks for ID values only.

If the SQL passes all checks, respond with exactly: "APPROVED: <the SQL>".
Otherwise, respond with exactly: "REJECTED: <specific, actionable feedback
explaining what must change>".
"""
        },

        # Security and compliance agent: checks access rights and injection risk
        # before the query is allowed to run.
        {
            "name": "security_and_compliance_agent",

            "function": {
                "description": "Checks a validated, alias-based SQL statement for access-control and SQL-injection risk before execution.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "sql": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The validated, alias-based SQL SELECT statement to check."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["sql"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
You are a security and compliance reviewer for database report queries.
Given an alias-based SQL SELECT statement that has already passed generation
and validation, check specifically for:
- SQL injection patterns (e.g. stacked queries, comment-based injection,
  tautologies like "1=1", UNION-based data exfiltration).
- Access to aliased fields whose explanations suggest sensitive data (e.g.
  passwords, tokens, social security numbers data) that should not
  appear in an ad hoc report unless explicitly and clearly requested.
- Any statement type other than a single read-only SELECT.

If the statement is safe and compliant, respond with exactly: "CLEARED".
Otherwise, respond with exactly: "BLOCKED: <reason>" describing the specific
security or compliance concern.
"""
        },

        # Down-chain CodedTool: the "Alias mapping (code, not LLM)" + "MCP
        # server" steps. It performs a deterministic sqlglot rewrite of the
        # alias-based SQL back to real names, then exposes the SQLite database
        # as a queryable tool.
        {
            "name": "sqlite_query_tool",
            "function": {
                "description": "Deterministically rewrites an alias-based SQL SELECT statement back to real table/column names (code, not LLM, via a sqlglot AST pass), then executes it against the SQLite report database and returns the resulting columns and rows.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "sql": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The alias-based, read-only SQL SELECT statement to execute (as produced by sql_generator_agent, using the aliases from schema_alias_tool)."
                        },
                        "db_path": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Optional absolute path to the SQLite database file. Omit this parameter to use the built-in default."
                        },
                        "schema_path": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Optional absolute path to the schema-alias JSON file used to map aliases back to real names. Omit this parameter to use the built-in default."
                        },
                        "row_limit": {
                            "type": "integer",
                            "description": "Optional maximum number of rows to return. Defaults to 1000."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["sql"]
                }
            },
            "class": "sqlite_query_tool.SqliteQueryTool"
        },

        # Down-chain CodedTool: the "Xlsx writer step", formatting query
        # results into a workbook for delivery.
        {
            "name": "xlsx_writer_tool",
            "function": {
                "description": "Formats tabular query results (columns and rows) into an .xlsx workbook and writes it to disk.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "columns": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "items": {"type": "string"},
                            "description": "Column headers for the report."
                        },
                        "rows": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "items": {
                                "type": "array",
                                "items": {"type": "string"}
                            },
                            "description": "Row data, each row being an array of cell values aligned with the columns."
                        },
                        "file_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Optional base file name (without extension) for the report. Defaults to a timestamped name."
                        },
                        "sheet_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Optional worksheet name. Defaults to 'Report'."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["columns", "rows"]
                }
            },
            "class": "xlsx_writer_tool.XlsxWriterTool"
        }
    ]
}
